Now, to understand the whole setup you will need to understand how I am planning on using the opcodes. Since I low key don't know anything, 
I relied on AI and after some back and forth came up with this:

R-Type: opcode 0000, funct 4 bits, 16 instructions:
  0000 0000  ADD    Rd = Rd + Rx, Sets Carry Flag
  0000 0001  SUB    Rd = Rd - Rx, Sets Neg or Equal Flag
  0000 0010  ADC    Rd = Rd + Rx + carry
  0000 0011  SBC    Rd = Rd - Rx - carry
  0000 0100  AND    Rd = Rd & Rx
  0000 0101  OR     Rd = Rd | Rx
  0000 0110  XOR    Rd = Rd ^ Rx
  0000 0111  NOT    Rd = ~Rx
  0000 1000  SLL    Rd = Rd << Rx
  0000 1001  SRL    Rd = Rd >> Rx
  0000 1010  SRA    Rd = Rd >> Rx
  0000 1011  CMP    Set Flags from Rd - Rx
  0000 1100  MOV    Rd = Rx
  0000 1101  NEG    Rd = -Rx
  0000 1110  LOAD   Rd = RAM[Rx]
  0000 1111  STORE  RAM[Rd] = Rx
  pseudo     POP    Rd = RAM[SP]
  pseudo     PUSH   RAM[SP] = Rx

I1-Type  opcodes 0001-0110, 5 instructions:
  0001       ADDI   Rd = Rd + imm8
  0010       SUBI   Rd = Rd - imm8
  0011       ANDI   Rd = Rd & imm8
  0100       ORI    Rd = Rd | imm8
  0101       XORI   Rd = Rd ^ imm8

I2-Type  opcodes 0110-1001, 4 instructions:
  0110       LDAT0  $T0 = RAM[addr12]
  0111       LDAT1  $T1 = RAM[addr12]
  1000       STAT0  RAM[addr12] = $T0
  1001       STAT1  RAM[addr12] = $T1

J-Type  opcodes 1010-1111, 6 instructions:
  1010       J      PC = imm12
  1011       JEQ    if zero:     PC = imm12
  1100       JNE    if !zero:    PC = imm12
  1101       JLT    if negative: PC = imm12
  1110       CALL   Move Value inside PC to hidden Return Stack, and increment Return Stack
  1111       RET    Move Value inside hidden Return Stack to PC, and decrement Return Stack

So, most of this is pretty self explanatory. But there are more types of instructions than MIPS, which is to get around some architecture 
challenges, the biggest one being how to move data from the registers to the SRAM modules on the board. The way I am planning on doing 
this is by using temp registers. The temp registers are special registers that the CPU uses for memory access. If a programmer wanted to 
read from memory, they would execute a read command to an address, then either store that data in a register for later, or use the temp 
register when making calculations. The I2 instructions will move data to these registers, $T0 and $T1, depening on the command. The 
values will be overwritten when the command is run, but otherwise are perfectly good registers.

Another thing to note is the LOAD and STORE instructions inside I1 instructions. You may notice they use an 8-bit IMM width to hold the 
memory address they want to store in, and that the addressable memory in this ISA is 12-bit. Firstly, I'd just like to say you're very
observent ;). But yes, this is correct behavior. The immediate address is limited, so the top 256 Bytes are informally designated as
the "Zero Page". This chunk of bytes are easier to get to thanks to this function. Instead of the two lines of:

  1 LDAT0 0x00F
  2 MOV $R0, $T0

We simply have:

  1 LOAD $R0, 0x00F

While this isn't a huge performance gain, it definetly reduces the amount of code you need for writes and reads, and when you're writing
a game loop that has 10-20 saves, the brevity is much appreciated. Now that you are thinking about the address specification, you might
as well have a look at it:

- 4KB Addressable Memory
- Includes Memory Mapped IO

# RAM (0x000 - 0x7FF, 2KB SRAM Chip)
    0x000 - 0x0FF    Zero-page RAM (256 bytes)
                     R-type LOAD/STORE addressable
                     Stack lives here ($SP initialized to 0x0FF)
    
    0x100 - 0x7FF    Extended RAM (~1.75KB)
                     I2-type only (LDAT0/LDAT1/STAT0/STAT1)
                     Larger data structures that do not need
                     fast register-indirect access
# Return Space
0x800 - 0x80F
0x810 - 0xFEF        Unassigned (reserved for expansion)

# GPU VRAM
    0xF?? - 0xF??    GPU sprite registers
    0xF?? - 0xFF9    GPU palette registers

# Sound Card
    0xFFA            AY-3-8910 sound chip address port
    0xFFB            AY-3-8910 sound chip data port

# Controller MMIO
    0xFFC            Controller Button Value
    0xFFD            Controller Joystick Y-Values
    0xFFE            Controller Joystick X-Values

0xFFF            System status / vertical blank flag. 
